# Architecture Decision Record (ADR)

ADR #: 005  
Title: Deployment Platform: Render  
Date: 2025-11-24  
Status: Proposed

## 1. Context

We need to host our Node.js/Express backend to serve APIs for our frontend (GitHub Pages) and external consumers.  
Key requirements:
- Simple, reliable public HTTPS endpoint for API calls.
- Auto-deployment triggered by changes to our GitHub repository.
- Easy management of environment variables (API tokens, secrets).
- Zero cost for MVP.
- Immediate error diagnosis, log access, and ability to roll back/redeploy quickly.

Current alternatives (GCP, Railway, self-hosted servers, AWS) require more manual setup, cloud expertise, and/or paid tiers for persistent reliability.

---

## Constraints / Requirements

- Must provide a free (or very low cost) tier for development and early production.
- Must support automatic builds and deployments from GitHub repo main branch with CI checks.
- Should support adding/changing environment variables via dashboard.
- Must make public API endpoints available over HTTPS.
- Should be easy to diagnose errors, view logs, and monitor deploys — no cloud or infrastructure skills required.
- Persistent disk is not needed for MVP; file storage via GitHub covers curated samples.

---

## Current state

Backend is structured as a Node.js/Express repo in `backend/`. No deployment provider was finalized.  
Requirements were defined by need for student/MVP velocity, cost savings, and frictionless auto-deployments.  
Render, Railway, and GCP were considered. Existing hosting (GitHub Pages) supports only frontend/static assets.

---

## 2. Decision

We choose **Render** for backend deployment.

- Render’s free tier supports Node.js web services, auto-builds from GitHub, instant HTTPS, and auto-sleep for inactive services.
- Push to `main` triggers auto-deploy, keeping releases in sync and enabling rapid experimentation.
- Dashboard provides flexible environment config for tokens, secrets, and keys, all updatable after deployment.
- Live logs and deploy rollback streamline debugging and fixes for team members.
- Suitable for MVP; comparable alternatives require higher effort or payment for same features.

**Why it solves our needs:**
- Fits our velocity and accessibility goals—any contributor can deploy, update env vars, and monitor in one place.
- Enables seamless integration with static frontend (GitHub Pages), since backend is public and CORS can be configured as needed.
- Reduces cognitive load compared to advanced cloud VMs or managed containers.

---

## 3. Alternatives Considered

### Google Cloud Platform (Cloud Run, App Engine, Compute Engine)
Pros: Supports advanced infrastructure, scaling, persistent disk, custom domains.
Cons: Heavier configuration for Node.js setup, network/firewall settings, environment, and CI/CD; free tier not easily persistent; higher learning curve for new contributors.

### Railway.app
Pros: Similar to Render, fast GitHub deployment, easy environment variable UI.
Cons: Free tier limited to lower memory and monthly compute hours; sleeps after inactivity; UI less intuitive than Render.

### Self-hosted VMs (AWS EC2, Azure, DigitalOcean, Linode)
Pros: Full OS and disk control, supports all backend and model serving needs.
Cons: Too complex for MVP; manual deploys, OS patching, SSL setup all required; not cost-effective for low/burst traffic.

### Netlify / Firebase / GitHub Pages
Pros: Perfect for frontend/static hosting; HTTPS and custom domain management.
Cons: Does not support Node.js/Express backend APIs.

---

## 4. Consequences

### Positive Outcomes
- Fast iteration and low-friction deployments.
- Free public API hosting for MVP and demos; can upgrade easily if needed.
- Dashboards make secrets management and troubleshooting accessible for all contributors.
- Great for projects with fluctuating or infrequent traffic.

### Negative Outcomes / Risks
- Service sleeps after 15 minutes of inactivity, may delay initial API response.
- Free plan limited in RAM/CPU; must upgrade for production scale.
- Lock-in risk: Migration to other providers requires new CI scripts or deploy config.

### Mitigations
- Document sleep/wake for frontend teams.
- Add alerts for backend errors/log monitoring.
- Plan migration path for larger traffic; update ADR if needed.

---

## 5. Implementation Notes

- Authorize Render to access GitHub repository for auto-deploy.
- Set service root to `/backend` and use `npm install`/`npm start` for build/run.
- Add env vars (`GITHUB_API_TOKEN`, etc.) in dashboard as needed.
- Confirm endpoint works and update frontend API URLs.
- Keep deployment docs with troubleshooting and rollback instructions.

